.TH JOT-CLI 1 "August 2026" "jot 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
jot\-cli \- on-device speech transcription utility (batch files and live streaming)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jot
.B transcribe
.I file
[\fB\-\-vtt\fR] [\fB\-\-diarize\fR] [\fB\-\-raw\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-vocab\fR] [\fB\-\-vocab\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-language\fR \fIcode\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIpath\fR] [\fB\-\-model\-dir\fR \fIdir\fR]
.br
.B jot
.B \-\-stream
[\fB\-\-language\fR \fIcode\fR] [\fB\-\-rate\fR \fI16000\fR] [\fB\-\-encoding\fR \fIs16le\fR|\fIf32le\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-vocab\fR] [\fB\-\-vocab\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-model\-dir\fR \fIdir\fR]
.br
.B jot
[\fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jot
transcribes speech entirely on device, using the same engines, cleanup
pipeline, and custom vocabulary as the Jot.app dictation utility. It ships
inside Jot.app and runs in parallel with it: the two never communicate at
runtime and share only on-disk conventions (the model directory and the
vocabulary file). Nothing is uploaded anywhere; no transcript is stored.
.PP
Two modes. \fBtranscribe\fR takes an audio or video file (anything the
bundled ffmpeg can decode: wav, mp3, m4a, webm, mkv, mp4, \[u2026]) and prints a
cleaned plain-text transcript to standard output. \fB\-\-stream\fR reads raw
PCM audio from standard input as it is produced and emits committed transcript
segments as newline-delimited JSON, one object per line, flushed per line \(em
built to be another program's ear on a live conversation.
.SH TRANSCRIBE MODE
Default output is plain text, run through the deterministic cleanup chain
(paragraph breaks from pause timing, filler-word removal, spoken-number
normalization, whitespace normalization \(em English-hardcoded stages are
skipped for other languages).
.TP
.B \-\-vtt
Emit WebVTT cues with timestamps instead of plain text.
.TP
.B \-\-diarize
Speaker-labeled WebVTT cues (implies \fB\-\-vtt\fR). Diarization is only
reliable on clean, separate-audio-per-voice input, such as a meeting
recording's own soundtrack \(em not audio captured acoustically through
speakers into a microphone.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Skip the cleanup chain. Vocabulary correction still applies unless
\fB\-\-no\-vocab\fR is also given.
.TP
.BI \-\-language " code"
Language hint for the engine and gating for the cleanup chain. Default
\fBen\fR. Bare codes (\fBes\fR, \fBde\fR, \fBzh\fR, \[u2026]) are accepted.
.TP
.BI \-o " path" "\fR,\fP \-\-output " path
Write to \fIpath\fR instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-model\-dir " dir"
Override the model root directory. See \fBFILES\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options; use before an input filename that begins with a dash.
.SH STREAM MODE
Input contract: raw PCM, 16 kHz, mono, on standard input, continuously, in
real time. \fBs16le\fR by default, \fBf32le\fR with \fB\-\-encoding\fR. A
leading RIFF/WAV header is detected and skipped; the \fB\-\-encoding\fR flag,
not the WAV header, governs sample decoding.
.PP
Output contract: one JSON object per line on standard output, flushed per
line:
.PP
.nf
    {"type":"final","text":"..."}
.fi
.PP
Each \fBfinal\fR is committed text, emitted as soon as the engine is
confident \(em segment by segment while the stream runs, not at the end. Text
is emitted exactly once; committed text is never revised. Nothing is emitted
during silence or non-speech: silence in, silence out. Fields are
additive-only; consumers must tolerate unknown fields.
.PP
The language is fixed for the whole stream via \fB\-\-language\fR (default
\fBen\fR). \fB\-\-rate\fR accepts only \fB16000\fR. On end-of-input (or
SIGINT) the engine flushes, the remaining text is emitted, and
.B jot
exits 0. If transcription fails mid-stream,
.B jot
prints the error to standard error and exits non-zero immediately: a dead
process is the intended failure signal, never a silent one.
.SH VOCABULARY
If the Jot.app custom vocabulary file exists (see \fBFILES\fR), transcripts
in both modes are corrected against it: term and alias matches are applied
through the same safety gate the app uses, so a custom term never silently
overwrites a word the engine clearly got right. This build uses the
model-free corrector (fuzzy text matching; no extra model downloads). The
app can additionally use an acoustic rescorer, so recall may differ slightly
between the two \(em same file, same gate, different detector.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-vocab
Disable vocabulary correction.
.TP
.BI \-\-vocab " file"
Use \fIfile\fR (same format: one term per line, optional comma-separated
aliases after a colon; \fB#\fR comments) instead of the default.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Jot/Models/Parakeet
Model root, shared with Jot.app (a convention, not a runtime link). The CLI
never downloads models: open Jot once to complete setup, then retry.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/Jot/Vocabulary/vocabulary.txt
Custom vocabulary, written by Jot.app's Settings \(-> Vocabulary pane and
read by both binaries.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure: undecodable input, models missing, engine error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Transcribe a file to the terminal, or into a file, or onto the clipboard:
.PP
.nf
    jot transcribe meeting.m4a
    jot transcribe meeting.m4a > notes.txt
    jot transcribe meeting.m4a | pbcopy
.fi
.PP
Subtitles with speakers from a meeting recording:
.PP
.nf
    jot transcribe standup.mp4 --diarize -o standup.vtt
.fi
.PP
Stream a file in real time (as if it were live):
.PP
.nf
    ffmpeg -re -i call.wav -f s16le -ar 16000 -ac 1 - | jot --stream
.fi
.PP
Live-transcribe microphone input via ffmpeg's capture device:
.PP
.nf
    ffmpeg -f avfoundation -i ":0" -f s16le -ar 16000 -ac 1 - | jot --stream
.fi
.PP
Feed an agent on a live call, Spanish, explicit wire format:
.PP
.nf
    your-call-audio | jot --stream --language es --rate 16000 --encoding s16le
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jot (1)
\(em the unrelated BSD sequence-printing utility that ships with macOS.
Jot.app's in-app Help covers the dictation side of the product.
